---
title: Testing & internals
description: Maintainer-facing test coverage, lifecycle invariants, and validation commands.
---

This page is for Spektral maintainers. Public consumers should rely on the domain guides and API reference.

## Coverage map

The package test suite covers:

- public root, core, adapter, and advanced exports;
- adapter context requirements, canvas props, error UI, and bounded history;
- scheduler ordering, stages, invalidation, replacement safety, timing, and profiling;
- material validation, preprocessing, immutability, caching, and signatures;
- uniform inference, validation, alignment, packing, and dirty ranges;
- texture normalization, upload modes, formats, mipmaps, loading, caching, aborts, and disposal;
- render-graph validation, named targets, pre-scene planning, post-scene routing, and color presentation;
- built-in render, compute, and fragment-feedback pass contracts and lifecycle;
- storage definitions, allocation, CPU transfers, bindings, and cleanup;
- structured error classification and shader source mapping;
- asynchronous fullscreen-pass preparation, hot-edit recovery, and stale-result isolation;
- immutable compute topology descriptors and physical-signature graph invalidation;
- frozen render-graph snapshots, cache identity, dependency reasons, and data isolation;
- core and runtime performance baselines, including compute bind-group pressure;
- Svelte, React, and Vue unit and E2E runtime parity.

## Runtime invariants

- Materials are immutable snapshots; declaration signatures ignore runtime uniform values.
- Uniform layouts and resource bindings use sorted keys for deterministic output.
- Runtime uniform uploads write dirty ranges rather than the complete user buffer.
- Texture bindings keep a valid fallback; allocation changes with size or format.
- Loaded bitmap disposal is idempotent and reloads cannot commit stale results.
- Render-pass objects are identity-tracked; optional `setSize` and `dispose` follow appearance, resize, removal, and teardown.
- Named render targets are signature-compared as `key:format:widthxheight`.
- Storage buffers use `STORAGE | COPY_DST | COPY_SRC`, flush pending writes before GPU work, and use disposable staging buffers for readback.
- Compute pipelines compile lazily. Pipeline and bind-group caches are reused while source and resource topology remain stable.
- Each compute pass owns one normalized, deeply frozen topology descriptor. Per-frame physical resource changes can refresh bindings and rebuild the graph plan without renormalizing that topology.
- Active built-in fullscreen pipelines are prepared asynchronously before the first frame. A shader hot edit keeps the last-known-good pipeline until the latest request succeeds; stale completions cannot replace newer source.
- Compute and feedback passes share the frame encoder but execute before the scene; render passes execute afterward.
- Fragment feedback owns A/B render textures, excludes its target from regular texture bindings, and attaches the latest view before the scene.
- The standard SDR path can render directly to canvas. Tone-mapped and HDR paths use an internal working surface and final presentation pass.
- Renderer teardown destroys owned GPU resources and its owned device.
- The experimental graph snapshot is deeply frozen and cached by plan identity. It uses opaque renderer-lifetime IDs and excludes GPU objects, WGSL, buffer data, callbacks, and timing records.

## Scheduler internals

The registry topologically sorts stages and tasks, uses an O(1) profiling ring buffer, and preallocates hot-path frame state. Diagnostics and profiling share one enable flag. Disabling either through the corresponding setter clears timing history.

Duplicate task keys replace the current registration atomically: cleanup from the replaced registration checks object identity before deleting the active task.

## Validation commands

From `packages/spektral`:

```bash
pnpm run build
pnpm run test
pnpm run check
pnpm run lint
pnpm run perf:core
pnpm run perf:core:check
pnpm run perf:runtime
pnpm run perf:runtime:check
pnpm run perf:gpu:check
pnpm run perf:renderer:check
pnpm run bundle:check
```

From `apps/web`:

```bash
pnpm run build
pnpm run check
pnpm run lint
```

Core, runtime, and bundle checks are deterministic enough for the normal quality flow. The strict
GPU and real-renderer commands are local release gates on the reference Apple M4 Pro/Metal machine;
they require matching GPU, backend, driver, macOS, Chromium major, and suite fingerprints. They are
not GitHub-hosted gates. Browser E2E coverage remains necessary for WebGPU behavior that DOM or GPU
mocks cannot prove.
